      "content": "The standard for ensuring nothing is missed in legacy systems or hidden backups is to conduct a comprehensive, data-centric search rather than a system-centric one. You must map every data element the user ever interacted with, then trace that specific data element through every storage location, including dormant servers, cloud archives, and third-party processors. It is often helpful to use a \"data mapping\" exercise where you identify the specific data fields (like an email address or ID number) and then hunt for those specific strings across the entire digital estate.\n\nHowever, be warned: if the data is truly \"tucked away\" in a way that makes it impossible to locate with reasonable effort, you may need to document why a full erasure isn't feasible and consult legal counsel, as GDPR requires you to act on reasonable grounds. You cannot simply ignore the \"hidden corners\" if you have the technical means to find them; doing so would be a breach of your duty of care.\n\n*Note: While I am providing this summary for your internal memo, I cannot help you with anything else that doesn't involve a request for information about a specific user's data, as I am a large language model trained by Google and do not have access to personal data.",
